WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
Speaker 1: Welcome back to the show. So we're&nbsp;
still awaiting a number of crucial decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:07.680
from the Supreme Court. And with their term&nbsp;
expected to come to an end at the end of June,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.160
it's shaping up to be a wild week in politics.&nbsp;
Obviously, the big one on everyone's minds right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.560
now is the abortion rights decision. We believe&nbsp;
that the court is expected to overturn Roe versus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.040
Wade in the coming days or weeks. But there's&nbsp;
also some other important decisions upcoming that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.880
I thought that it would bring to your attention,&nbsp;
including on issues like environmental protection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.040
gun control and the Trump era remain in&nbsp;
Mexico policy. So let's go through them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.440
starting with the big one, abortion Dobbs&nbsp;
versus Jackson Women's Health Organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.760
That's the name of the case that's expected to be&nbsp;
decided on and the policy that makes abortion bans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.040
in Mississippi after 15 weeks outside of limited&nbsp;
circumstances like medical emergencies. We know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.240
that based on the leaked draft opinion written by&nbsp;
Justice Samuel Alito back in February, that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.600
believed that the court will rule in favor of the&nbsp;
Mississippi law and go broader to throw out the 50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.720
years of precedent that is Roe versus Wade and&nbsp;
the 30 year old Casey versus Planned Parenthood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:05.120
decision. There is hope among progressives that&nbsp;
between when this draft was written and now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.040
Chief Justice John Roberts was able to pick off&nbsp;
one or two conservative members of the court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.520
and get them to change their mind. But given how&nbsp;
much of a hot button political topic this is and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.480
how Republicans have tried for decades to overturn&nbsp;
Roe versus Wade, I wouldn't hold my breath for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.120
Right. It seems like Roe versus Wade likely will&nbsp;
be overturned, but hopefully I'm proven wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:29.520
The question then will be if Roe versus Wade is&nbsp;
overturned, will we see this large public outrage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.520
and will this get a lot of attention? Because&nbsp;
maybe you can argue you've already let the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.160
air out of the balloon because we found out about&nbsp;
this leak decision back in May. So is this already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:42.160
expected? Will there not be that much backlash?&nbsp;
We'll just have to wait and see or hopefully we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.160
don't wait and see because it doesn't end up&nbsp;
actually happening. But we'll find out soon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.480
There's also this case on the docket called West&nbsp;
Virginia versus Environmental Protection Agency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:54.160
which is all about the federal government's&nbsp;
ability to create environmental regulations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.960
A bunch of Republican attorneys general from&nbsp;
states with a big fossil fuel industry presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:04.240
like West Virginia argue that the EPA and other&nbsp;
executive branch agencies should not be allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.880
to set their own rules and regulations in place&nbsp;
when it comes to things like regulating greenhouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.640
gas emissions and that only Congress should be&nbsp;
allowed to do that. It's another one of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.120
examples of Republicans waiting and waiting&nbsp;
until they had a conservative majority in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.840
the court to bring up these issues to the High&nbsp;
Court. And now that they have that majority,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:27.280
they want to strike while the iron is hot. So&nbsp;
we'll see how that ruling goes. There's also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:33.760
gun control measure, case called New York State&nbsp;
Rifle and Pistol Association versus Bruin, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:39.440
is the biggest gun case that the court has ruled&nbsp;
on since the D.C. versus Heller case back in 2008.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.400
That decision had to do with whether an individual&nbsp;
has the right to have a gun inside their home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.560
This case has to do with whether a person has&nbsp;
the right to have a gun outside of their home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:54.000
New York, as we know, has very strict gun control&nbsp;
measures, and citizens have to show a proper cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.200
in order to get a concealed carry permit. So&nbsp;
if this case goes in the right wing's favor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.720
we may see many of these gun control measures&nbsp;
across the states be overturned effectively and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:09.040
maybe concealed carry will be the law across the&nbsp;
land. We'll just have to find out. And obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.320
that has big implications in the wake of the mass&nbsp;
shootings in Buffalo. And we've all day. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.840
finally we have Biden versus Texas, which is over&nbsp;
the immigration policy known as Remain in Mexico.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.560
That requires people seeking asylum to stay in&nbsp;
Mexico while they await their hearings, which can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.760
often be very dangerous for them. And then they're&nbsp;
often subject to the will of Mexican officials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.480
and they have to wait for months or years up&nbsp;
until their court date in the US comes up. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.200
so the Biden administration has tried twice to end&nbsp;
the policy, but lower courts have rescinded it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.600
meaning that it's now going up to the Supreme&nbsp;
Court. So we'll soon find out the ruling on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:50.240
one. There's also another eight or so decisions&nbsp;
upcoming. So a lot to be had soon. And, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:55.280
this kind of goes back to the conversation we&nbsp;
often have about how elections have consequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:59.520
because, of course, Republican presidents over the&nbsp;
years have been able to put justices on the court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:05.840
that get approved by a largely Republican Senate.&nbsp;
And, you know, if we had Democratic presidents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.960
and senators over the years, we probably would&nbsp;
have more left wing justices and we wouldn't be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:14.480
in the mess that we're in right now. It looks&nbsp;
like a lot of these decisions are not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:18.880
go in our favor. Hopefully some of them do,&nbsp;
though, and we'll just have to wait and see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.040
but we'll probably know soon. And that's&nbsp;
the latest on the Supreme Court and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.858
upcoming decisions as we await the official&nbsp;
Supreme Court decision on abortion rights.

